--- /candidate/PROOF.md
     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# Proof report
     4	
     5	## What is proved
     6	
     7	`solution.py` implements `anti_shuffle(s)` by scanning the input once at the
     8	outer level, maintaining the current word in sorted order by insertion. A
     9	literal space (code point 32) terminates the current word and is copied to the
    10	output. Thus every maximal space-delimited word has its characters sorted in
    11	ascending Python/K string order, while every separator and its position among
    12	the words is preserved.
    13	
    14	The required target theorem is symbolic over `INPUT:IntSeq`. It therefore
    15	covers every finite string represented by the supplied semantics, with no
    16	bound on input length, number or length of words, number of spaces, or
    17	character code values. The K claims establish partial correctness under the
    18	supplied reference semantics.
    19	
    20	## Formal claims
    21	
    22	`spec.k` contains three reachability claims, all proved together:
    23	
    24	1. `anti-shuffle-inner-loop` summarizes one invocation of the inner insertion
    25	   loop for arbitrary `W:IntSeq`, inserted character `C:Int`, accumulated
    26	   prefix `N:IntSeq`, insertion flag `I:Bool`, surrounding continuation
    27	   `K:K`, and stack `STACK:List`.
    28	2. `anti-shuffle-outer-loop` summarizes the outer scan for arbitrary remaining
    29	   input `S:IntSeq`, completed result `R:IntSeq`, and current word `W:IntSeq`.
    30	3. `anti-shuffle` loads the exact generated module, resolves and calls
    31	   `anti_shuffle`, and proves that arbitrary `INPUT:IntSeq` returns
    32	   `str(antiGo(INPUT, .IntSeq, .IntSeq))`.
    33	
    34	The recursive mathematical summaries in `verification.k` are:
    35	
    36	- `insertGo(W, C, N, I)`: scan `W`, copy every old character, and insert `C`
    37	  immediately before the first strictly greater character, or append it if no
    38	  such character exists.
    39	- `antiGo(S, R, W)`: scan `S`; on code point 32 append `W` and one space to
    40	  `R` and reset `W`; otherwise replace `W` with
    41	  `insertGo(W, C, .IntSeq, false)`. At end, append `W`.
    42	
    43	Starting each word with `.IntSeq`, structural induction on the equations for
    44	`insertGo` shows that each consumed character is inserted exactly once,
    45	existing characters are copied exactly once, and the result is ascending.
    46	Structural induction on `antiGo` then shows that non-space characters are
    47	partitioned into the same words, each word is sorted, and every literal space
    48	is copied exactly once in its original separator position. This is the full
    49	HumanEval contract, including empty strings and repeated, leading, and
    50	trailing spaces.
    51	
    52	## Proof-extension inventory
    53	
    54	| Extension | Kind and domain | Coverage / overlap | State and control effect | Value influence | Connection to fixed semantics |
    55	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    56	| `solutionModule()`, `solutionBody()` | Generated, zero-argument definitional aliases for the complete translated program and function body | Exact single definitions | None; syntax construction only | Selects the actual program executed by the entry theorem | `generate_program_module.py` derives them from `solution.py` through the fixed `py2mpy.py` translator; the entry claim executes them through the supplied loader and call rules |
    57	| `antiInnerBody()`, `antiPostInsert()`, `antiOuterBody()`, `antiTail()` | Definitional aliases for exact AST subterms | Exact single definitions | None; syntax construction only | Identify loop heads and residual statements used in auxiliary claims | Their expansions are the generated program subterms; fixed MPY rules perform all execution |
    58	| `insertGo(IntSeq, Int, IntSeq, Bool)` | Total definitional summary | Empty/cons cases are exhaustive and disjoint; recursion decreases the first `IntSeq`; the conditional partitions all Boolean/order cases | None | Computes the claimed post-loop locals | `anti-shuffle-inner-loop` proves fixed-semantics execution from the exact inner-loop head to those locals for arbitrary symbolic inputs and framed continuation |
    59	| `antiGo(IntSeq, IntSeq, IntSeq)` | Total definitional summary | Empty/cons cases are exhaustive and disjoint; recursion decreases the first `IntSeq`; `C ==Int 32` and its complement partition the cons case | None | Computes the target return value | `anti-shuffle-outer-loop` and `anti-shuffle` prove fixed-semantics execution agrees with it for arbitrary symbolic inputs |
    60	| `strLt(iCons(C,.IntSeq), iCons(D,.IntSeq)) => C <Int D` | Derived simplification lemma on singleton-character strings | Domain is exactly the singleton strings produced by string iteration; less/greater/equal cases are exhaustive and disjoint | None | Determines the insertion branch condition | `lemma-spec.k`, compiled against the unextended reference definition, proves all three order cases with `#Top` |
    61	| Inner and outer loop claims | Auxiliary circularity reachability theorems | Symbolic and unbounded over every loop input/state parameter shown above | The inner claim frames arbitrary continuation and stack; the outer claim checks the exact return/pop configuration | Establish the two summaries used by the entry proof | They are K claims, not operational rewrites; both close under the fixed semantics |
    62	
    63	There are no operational bridge rules, trusted primitives, opaque evaluators,
    64	host-language calls, or axioms that replace program execution. The only
    65	executable extension rule is the independently proved singleton `strLt`
    66	simplifier. The other additions are constructors, equations for specification
    67	functions, or proof claims.
