   119	
   120	## Gate results
   121	
   122	### Gate A — proof-mechanism soundness: PASS
   123	
   124	- **A1, body connection:** the target starts with the exact mechanically
   125	  generated module, uses the supplied module loader/name lookup/call
   126	  machinery, and reaches the generated function body. A body-only mutation
   127	  invalidates the proof.
   128	- **A2, state and control connection:** no extension performs Python
   129	  execution. Claims expose the relevant `<k>`, environment, scopes, heap,
   130	  heap-location, call stack, return, exception, and exit cells. Heap remains
   131	  empty for this allocation-free implementation, and returns pass through the
   132	  fixed call/return rules.
   133	- **A3, primitive and dispatch connection:** loading, dispatch, binding,
   134	  iteration, assignment, comparison, and return are executed by the supplied
   135	  MPY semantics. The sole comparison simplifier is independently proved
   136	  against that unextended semantics.
   137	- **A4, equation discipline:** specification equations are total,
   138	  structurally decreasing, and split into disjoint empty/cons and Boolean
   139	  cases. No competing overlaps choose incompatible results.
   140	- **A5, vacuity resistance:** the empty-input false-target probe fails, and a
   141	  generated-body semantic mutation also fails.
   142	
   143	### Gate B — theorem relevance to the full contract: PASS
   144	
   145	- The entry claim is unbounded over every finite `IntSeq`; there is no finite
   146	  test-size assumption or loop unrolling bound.
   147	- The postcondition is the exact recursive meaning of sorting each
   148	  space-delimited word and preserving every literal space.
   149	- Empty input, one or many words, already sorted and reverse-sorted words,
   150	  duplicate characters, punctuation, mixed case, and repeated/edge spaces are
   151	  all included symbolically.
   152	- The symbolic theorem imposes no character-code restriction. The supplied
   153	  concrete source-string parser has an ASCII-oriented literal boundary, but
   154	  that parser limitation is not a precondition of the symbolic `INPUT:IntSeq`
   155	  theorem. Independent CPython tests include non-ASCII strings.
   156	
   157	### Gate C — validation strength: PASS
   158	
   159	- The independent oracle uses Python's `split(" ")`, per-word `sorted`, and
   160	  `join`, rather than either K summary function. It checked all strings of
   161	  length at most five over `" aB!z"`, the prompt examples and edge cases, and
   162	  2,000 deterministic randomized ASCII/Unicode strings.
   163	- A false postcondition and a source-body mutation both fail, demonstrating
   164	  that the positive proof is neither vacuous nor detached from the submitted
   165	  implementation.
   166	- The comparison extension is validated separately against the frozen
   167	  semantics in all order cases.
   168	
   169	## Trust boundary
   170	
   171	The trusted base is the supplied `reference-semantics/`, K's generated proof
   172	machinery and backends, the K toolchain itself, and the fixed `py2mpy.py`
   173	translation scheme. `generate_program_module.py` only packages that
   174	translator's output as named K syntax aliases; the mutation probe audits that
   175	the resulting body remains proof-relevant.
   176	
   177	No proof-local function is treated as a trusted implementation of the Python
   178	program. `insertGo` and `antiGo` are specification functions whose agreement
   179	with actual MPY execution is proved by the reachability claims. The report
   180	does not claim correctness of the reference semantics relative to all of
   181	CPython, nor total correctness/termination beyond the partial-correctness
   182	meaning of the K reachability proof.
   183	
   184	## Empirically supported facts
   185	
   186	The executable implementation matches the independent CPython oracle on 5,847
   187	cases, including the original examples, whitespace edge cases, mixed
   188	punctuation/case, duplicate characters, and Unicode samples. Concrete LLVM
   189	execution of translated assertions also succeeds. These checks support the
   190	model boundary and implementation intent; the unbounded coverage statement
   191	comes from the symbolic theorem, not from testing.
   192	
   193	## Excluded behavior
   194	
   195	The HumanEval signature and contract require a string input. Behavior for
   196	non-string Python objects is outside the theorem. The proof treats a separator
   197	as the literal space character only; tabs and newlines are ordinary sortable
   198	word characters, matching the contract's wording and examples. Exceptions,
   199	resource exhaustion, implementation-defined Unicode collation beyond
   200	code-point ordering, and equivalence of the supplied partial MPY semantics to
   201	full CPython are not claimed.
